# DTF tier manifest: audit-event-coverage  (artifact-keeper#4710 / #4718)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a webhook secret-encryption key. The audit trail is
# written by the API handlers into Postgres, ahead of any storage choice, so
# the storage dimension is irrelevant. secrets.webhook-key only exists so the
# webhook rotate-secret call succeeds (without a key it answers 500 and no
# rotation, audited or not, can happen).
#
# THE BUGS
# #4710: the quarantine, approval, promotion, permission-rule, SSO-admin,
# signing-key, webhook and sync-policy handlers wrote NO audit_log rows. An
# auditor could not answer who released a held package, approved or ran a
# promotion, granted a permission, changed an identity provider, rotated a key
# or repointed a webhook. Fixed by artifact-keeper#4716, which records each
# action with the acting principal (`user_id`, `actor`, verified client IP via
# `AuditEntry::for_principal`), the target as `resource_id`, and secret-free
# `details`.
# #4718: `audit_retention_days` was applied only by the manual
# `POST /api/v1/admin/cleanup`; nothing ran on a schedule, the settings
# endpoint advertised a `90` that was never applied, and the manual cleanup
# used that unsaved 90 (and `0` meant "delete every row"). Fixed by
# artifact-keeper#4720: an hourly scheduler pass (first run 240-270 s after
# startup) deletes rows older than a saved positive period; unsaved or `0`
# means retention is off, the settings endpoint reports `0`, and the manual
# cleanup deletes nothing in that state. The period is a system setting, not
# an env var, so this manifest sets nothing for it: the oracle saves the
# smallest legal period (1 day) through the API as its first action, ahead of
# the first pass.
#
# THE PRIMITIVES (each through the real admin API, as the bootstrap admin
# unless noted)
#   POST/PUT/DELETE /api/v1/permissions[/{id}]          PERMISSION_RULE_*
#   POST /api/v1/webhooks, /{id}/disable, /{id}/rotate-secret, DELETE /{id}
#                                                       WEBHOOK_*
#   POST/PUT/DELETE /api/v1/admin/sso/oidc[/{id}], PATCH /{id}/toggle
#                                                       SSO_PROVIDER_*
#   POST /api/v1/signing/keys/{id}/rotate, /{id}/revoke SIGNING_KEY_*
#   POST /api/v1/quarantine/{artifact}/release|reject   QUARANTINE_*
#   POST /api/v1/promotion/repositories/{stg}/artifacts/{a}/promote|reject
#        (and a promote a min_staging_hours rule refuses) ARTIFACT_PROMOTED,
#                                                       PROMOTION_REJECTED,
#                                                       PROMOTION_REFUSED
#   POST /api/v1/approval/{id}/approve|reject, by a SECOND admin (four-eyes)
#                                                       PROMOTION_APPROVAL_*
#   POST/PUT/DELETE /api/v1/sync-policies[/{id}]        SYNC_POLICY_*
#   GET/POST /api/v1/admin/settings, POST /api/v1/admin/cleanup
#   UPDATE audit_log SET created_at = ... (backdating rows the oracle created)
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL)  true on BOTH images: repository create writes exactly one
#              REPOSITORY_CREATED row and user create exactly one USER_CREATED
#              row, both attributed to the admin; a login carrying a spoofed
#              X-Forwarded-For records the TCP peer, not the spoof (no trusted
#              proxy is configured, the 1.11.1 #4681 contract); a saved
#              audit_retention_days reads back.
#   (FIX #4710) every primitive above writes exactly one row with the
#              expected action, resource_type and resource_id (DB count and
#              the GET /api/v1/admin/audit filter both say 1), attributed to
#              the acting admin with the same peer IP the login row recorded;
#              no row carries the OIDC client secret, the webhook secret, or
#              the webhook URL's userinfo/query. RED on 1.11.1: count 0.
#   (FIX #4718) the unsaved period reads 0 (1.11.1: 90); a manual cleanup with
#              no saved period deletes nothing (1.11.1: deletes a 100-day-old
#              row under the unsaved 90); after the first scheduled pass, rows
#              backdated 2, 30 and 100 days are gone while a 23-hour-old row
#              and every fresh row this run wrote survive (1.11.1: no pass
#              ever runs, the backdated rows stay).
#
# The scheduled-pass wait is bounded by the container start time + 390 s, so
# the tier takes four and a half minutes on the fixed image and six and a half
# on one with no scheduled pass.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: two admin logins and ~80 back-to-back
# authenticated management calls; the base default (ON) would throttle them
# and turn an assertion into a 429.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem secrets.webhook-key"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
# The webhook secret-encryption key profiles/secrets.webhook-key.yml needs
# (base64 of a fixed 32-byte non-production ASCII string). Derived here at run
# time so the repository holds no key-shaped literal; run.sh sources this file
# before `docker compose up`, so the export reaches the compose interpolation.
export DTF_WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY="$(printf 'dtf-audit-event-coverage-key-32b' | base64)"
